/**
 * Pediatric visit coding guide.
 *
 * WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS
 * Keyword Planner shows a real cluster here — `cpt 99394` and `cpt code 99394` at
 * 100–1K/month, with roughly eight more pediatric visit-code terms at 10–100. Unlike
 * most of the volume in this space, the searchers are the buyer: pediatric clinicians
 * and their billers. Compare `pm pediatrics billing`, which is a parent paying a bill.
 *
 * It is also not an orphan. The modifier 25 case below — a separately billable problem
 * inside a preventive visit — is the pediatric form of the scenario The Undercoding Tax
 * opens with, and the documentation standard comes from The Defensible Visit.
 *
 * NO AMA DESCRIPTORS ARE REPRODUCED HERE, DELIBERATELY.
 * The code numbers are freely usable; the official CPT long descriptors are AMA
 * copyright and licensed separately. River Records licenses CPT for use *in the
 * product*, which is not automatically a licence to publish descriptors on an open
 * marketing page — those are usually distinct grants with their own attribution
 * requirements. So every code here is described in our own words and the authoritative
 * text is linked rather than copied. If the licence is later confirmed to cover
 * publication, descriptors and the required `CPT © AMA` attribution can be added; the
 * page does not depend on them.
 *
 * ACCURACY BAR: every coding claim traces to AAP or AMA guidance. Age bands and the
 * three-year rule were verified against AAP and AAPC sources before writing. A
 * physician should read this before any material change.
 */
import Page from '../../../layouts/Page.astro';

const established = [
  { code: '99391', band: 'Infant', detail: 'Younger than 1 year' },
  { code: '99392', band: 'Early childhood', detail: 'Ages 1 through 4' },
  { code: '99393', band: 'Late childhood', detail: 'Ages 5 through 11' },
  { code: '99394', band: 'Adolescent', detail: 'Ages 12 through 17' },
  { code: '99395', band: 'Young adult', detail: 'Ages 18 through 39' },
];
const newPt = [
  { code: '99381', band: 'Infant', detail: 'Younger than 1 year' },
  { code: '99382', band: 'Early childhood', detail: 'Ages 1 through 4' },
  { code: '99383', band: 'Late childhood', detail: 'Ages 5 through 11' },
  { code: '99384', band: 'Adolescent', detail: 'Ages 12 through 17' },
  { code: '99385', band: 'Young adult', detail: 'Ages 18 through 39' },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is CPT 99394?',
    a: 'The periodic preventive medicine visit for an established patient aged 12 through 17 — the adolescent well visit. Its new-patient equivalent is 99384.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the CPT code for a pediatric well visit?',
    a: 'It depends on the child’s age and whether they are new or established. Established patients: 99391 (under 1), 99392 (1–4), 99393 (5–11), 99394 (12–17). New patients use 99381–99384 across the same age bands.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What counts as a new versus an established patient?',
    a: 'The standard three-year rule. A patient is new if they have not received a face-to-face service from you, or from another clinician of the same specialty in your group, within the past three years.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are preventive medicine codes selected by time?',
    a: 'No. Unlike office visit codes since 2021, preventive medicine codes are selected by patient age and new-versus-established status, not by time or medical decision-making. Time may still be documented but does not drive the code.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you bill a sick visit and a well visit on the same day?',
    a: 'Yes, when the problem is significant and separately identifiable — enough additional work that it could stand alone as an office visit. The problem-oriented visit is reported alongside the preventive service with modifier 25 appended, and the documentation must support each independently, with no part of the preventive exam doing double duty.',
  },
];
